ASSIGNMENT 8: DISK SCHEDULING — SSTF, SCAN, C-LOOK
-----------------------------------------------------------

Aim:
To implement disk scheduling algorithms and calculate the total head movement.

Algorithms:
1. SSTF (Shortest Seek Time First):
   Selects the pending request closest to the current disk head position.
   It generally reduces seek movement, but requests may starve.

2. SCAN:
   The disk head moves in one direction servicing requests, then reverses direction.
   It is also called the elevator algorithm.
   The discussed code moves toward cylinder 0 first and then services requests in the other direction. It uses a simplified boundary handling, so explain the actual code behavior in the practical.

3. C-LOOK:
   The head services requests in one direction up to the last request in that direction, then jumps to the farthest request on the other side and continues.
   It does not travel all the way to the physical disk boundary unless a request is there.

Important terms:
- Seek time: time required for the disk head to move to a track/cylinder.
- Head movement: distance traveled by the disk head.
- Total head movement: sum of the distances between successive head positions.
- Disk scheduling aims to reduce seek time and improve performance.

